---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import DocsSidebar from "../../components/DocsSidebar.astro";
import CodeWindow from "../../components/CodeWindow.astro";
import PropsTable from "../../components/PropsTable.astro";
import JsApiDemo from "../../components/JsApiDemo.astro";
import "../../styles/docs.css";
---

<Layout title="JavaScript API — Motion Components" description="Play, pause, finish and cancel any motion component from JavaScript, wait for it to end, listen for its events, and control every component at once." noMain>
  <div class="layout">
    <DocsSidebar />

    <main id="main">
      <h1><motion-split by="chars" interval="0.025" duration="0.5">JavaScript API</motion-split></h1>
      <p class="lead">Every component is an HTML element, so you control it the way you control any element: find it, call a method, listen for an event.</p>

      <h2>Quick start</h2>
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.js"
        copy
        code={`const reveal = document.querySelector('motion-reveal')

reveal.play()        // start, or resume after pause()
reveal.pause()       // hold where it is
reveal.finish()      // jump to the end
reveal.cancel()      // stop and go back to the start
reveal.replay()      // go back to the start and play again

reveal.playState     // 'idle' | 'running' | 'paused' | 'finished'
await reveal.finished   // wait for the current run to end`}
      />
      <p>Try the methods on a ticker:</p>
      <JsApiDemo target="api-ticker" wide>
        <motion-ticker id="api-ticker" speed="60">
          <span class="api-chip"><code>play()</code> starts or resumes</span>
          <span class="api-chip"><code>pause()</code> holds in place</span>
          <span class="api-chip"><code>finish()</code> jumps to the end</span>
          <span class="api-chip"><code>cancel()</code> resets to the start</span>
          <span class="api-chip"><code>replay()</code> plays again</span>
          <span class="api-chip"><code>playState</code> says where it is</span>
          <span class="api-chip"><code>finished</code> waits for the end</span>
        </motion-ticker>
      </JsApiDemo>

      <h2>Methods and properties</h2>
      <PropsTable
        nameLabel="Member"
        rows={[
          { name: "play()", type: "() => Promise<void>", default: "—", description: "Starts the animation, or resumes it after <code>pause()</code>. Does nothing while it runs. The promise resolves when the run ends." },
          { name: "pause()", type: "() => void", default: "—", description: "Holds the animation where it is. <code>play()</code> continues it." },
          { name: "finish()", type: "() => void", default: "—", description: "Jumps to the end state." },
          { name: "cancel()", type: "() => void", default: "—", description: "Stops the animation and returns to the start state." },
          { name: "replay()", type: "() => void", default: "—", description: "Returns to the start and plays again. Available on components with an animation that ends, such as the reveals, text effects, charts, icons and <code>motion-code</code>." },
          { name: "playState", type: "PlaybackState", default: "'idle'", description: "<code>'idle'</code>, <code>'running'</code>, <code>'paused'</code> or <code>'finished'</code>." },
          { name: "finished", type: "Promise<void>", default: "—", description: "Resolves when the current run ends, by finishing or by <code>cancel()</code>. Each run gets a new promise. It never rejects." },
        ]}
      />
      <p class="note"><code>motion-glitch</code> also has <code>glitch()</code>, which fires one extra burst.</p>

      <h2>Wait for an animation</h2>
      <p><code>play()</code> returns a promise, so you can run code after an animation ends.</p>
      <JsApiDemo target="api-reveal" mode="one-shot" wide>
        <motion-reveal id="api-reveal" duration="0.8" y="32">
          <div class="demo-card">Triggered from JavaScript</div>
        </motion-reveal>
      </JsApiDemo>
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.js"
        copy
        code={`const reveal = document.querySelector('motion-reveal')

reveal.cancel()
await reveal.play()
console.log('reveal complete')`}
      />
      <p class="note">An endless effect, such as <code>motion-ticker</code>, <code>motion-circle</code> or any component with <code>loop</code>, never ends by itself. Its promise resolves only when you call <code>finish()</code> or <code>cancel()</code>, so don't await it.</p>

      <h2 id="events">Events</h2>
      <p>Components fire these events. They bubble and cross shadow roots, so one listener on <code>document</code> hears every component on the page.</p>
      <PropsTable
        nameLabel="Event"
        rows={[
          { name: "motion-start", type: "CustomEvent", default: "—", description: "A run starts, from its trigger or from <code>play()</code>." },
          { name: "motion-finish", type: "CustomEvent", default: "—", description: "A run reaches its end, or <code>finish()</code> jumps there." },
          { name: "motion-cancel", type: "CustomEvent", default: "—", description: "<code>cancel()</code> stops a run and resets it." },
        ]}
      />
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.js"
        copy
        code={`document.addEventListener('motion-finish', (event) => {
  console.log(event.target.localName, 'finished')
})`}
      />
      <p>Some components contain others. <code>motion-gallery</code> uses <code>motion-stagger</code> and <code>motion-hover</code>, so a listener on the gallery also hears the stagger's events. When you listen on an element that contains other components, check <code>event.target</code> first.</p>
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.js"
        copy
        code={`const card = document.querySelector('#card')

card.addEventListener('motion-finish', (event) => {
  if (event.target !== card) return
  console.log('the card finished')
})`}
      />

      <h2>Control every component at once</h2>
      <p>Import these helpers to pause, resume or cancel every component on the page, or only the ones inside an element you pass.</p>
      <JsApiDemo target="pause-all-scope" mode="pause-all" wide>
        <div id="pause-all-scope">
          <div class="loop-row">
            <motion-circle text="orbit · orbit · orbit ·" duration="14"></motion-circle>
            <motion-typewriter loop>typing along…</motion-typewriter>
          </div>
        </div>
      </JsApiDemo>
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.js"
        copy
        code={`import { pauseAll, resumeAll } from 'motion-components'

// Pause everything while the tab is hidden
document.addEventListener('visibilitychange', () => {
  document.hidden ? pauseAll() : resumeAll()
})

// Or only the components inside one element
pauseAll(document.querySelector('#hero'))`}
      />
      <PropsTable
        nameLabel="Export"
        rows={[
          { name: "pauseAll(root?)", type: "(root?: Node) => void", default: "document", description: "Pauses every running component inside <code>root</code>, and disables the components that follow the pointer." },
          { name: "resumeAll(root?)", type: "(root?: Node) => void", default: "document", description: "Resumes what <code>pauseAll()</code> paused, and enables only what it disabled." },
          { name: "cancelAll(root?)", type: "(root?: Node) => void", default: "document", description: "Cancels every component inside <code>root</code>." },
        ]}
      />
      <p class="note"><code>pauseAll()</code> also reaches components inside other components, such as the stagger inside a gallery.</p>

      <h2>TypeScript</h2>
      <p>Each component registers its tag, so <code>querySelector</code> returns the right type without a cast. Use <code>MotionControllable</code> for code that works with any component.</p>
      <CodeWindow
        filename="app.ts"
        copy
        code={`import type { MotionControllable, PlaybackState } from 'motion-components'

const reveal = document.querySelector('motion-reveal')   // MotionReveal | null

function restart(el: MotionControllable) {
  el.cancel()
  void el.play()
}

const state: PlaybackState | undefined = reveal?.playState`}
      />

      <h2>Reduced motion</h2>
      <p>When the visitor asks for reduced motion, <code>play()</code> shows the end state at once. It still resolves, and it still fires <code>motion-start</code> and <code>motion-finish</code>, so your code needs no special case. <code>motion-words</code> and <code>motion-countdown</code> keep running without animation, because changing words and counting are their job.</p>

      <h2>Scroll-driven components</h2>
      <p><code>motion-parallax</code>, <code>motion-scene</code>, <code>motion-progress</code> and <code>motion-blur</code> follow the scroll position instead of a clock. <code>playState</code> is <code>'running'</code> while they follow it. <code>pause()</code> stops following and holds the current state, <code>play()</code> follows again, <code>finish()</code> shows the end state, and <code>cancel()</code> stops and resets.</p>

      <h2 id="without-playback">Components without these methods</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Pointer-driven:</strong> <code>motion-hover</code>, <code>motion-press</code>, <code>motion-magnetic</code> and <code>motion-tilt</code> follow the pointer. Set <code>disabled</code> to turn them off; <code>pauseAll()</code> does it for you. <code>motion-stretch</code> also follows the pointer, but has no <code>disabled</code>.</li>
        <li><strong>Widgets:</strong> <code>motion-dialog</code>, <code>motion-gallery</code>, <code>motion-slider</code>, <code>motion-flip-card</code>, <code>motion-image-compare</code>, <code>motion-spotlight</code>, <code>motion-icon-state</code>, <code>motion-theme-icon</code> and <code>motion-theme-toggle</code> have their own methods, such as <code>show()</code> on the dialog and <code>goTo()</code> on the slider. Each component's page lists them.</li>
        <li><strong>Static:</strong> <code>motion-code-inline</code> does not animate.</li>
      </ul>
    </main>
  </div>
</Layout>

<script>
  import "motion-components/motion-circle";
  import "motion-components/motion-reveal";
  import "motion-components/motion-split";
  import "motion-components/motion-ticker";
  import "motion-components/motion-typewriter";
</script>

<style>
  .demo-card {
    background: var(--mc-color-surface-2);
    border: 1px solid var(--mc-color-border);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 2rem;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--mc-color-text);
    text-align: center;
  }

  #api-ticker {
    -webkit-mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, #000 8%, #000 92%, transparent);
    mask-image: linear-gradient(to right, transparent, #000 8%, #000 92%, transparent);
  }

  .api-chip {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    padding: 0.5rem 0.9rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--mc-color-border);
    border-radius: 999px;
    background: var(--mc-color-surface-2);
    color: var(--mc-color-muted);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .api-chip code {
    color: var(--mc-color-text);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .loop-row {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 1.5rem;
    font-weight: 600;
  }
</style>
